---
// "Senaste nytt" — the front page's sidolinje.
//
// A narrow column of what has happened, modelled on the Swedish news-day index
// (Sydsvenskan's *Nyhetsdygnet* is the reference): a time, a category, a
// headline, and nothing else. No summary, no source, no picture. It is meant to
// be run down with the eye in a second, not read.
//
// Every headline links INWARD, to that notice on /nyheter/. The news is read on
// islam.se; zuhd.news is credited once at the foot of the column and once more
// on /nyheter/, and that is the whole of it. An earlier draft put an outbound
// link on every row, which turned the front page into six exits to a site the
// reader had never heard of, under a label — »Se på kartan« — that named a
// mechanism nobody could see.
//
// ⚠️ That credit IS the one outbound link, and it is meant to be. The rule above
// is one exit, not none: a provenance note that names a publication without
// letting the reader go and look at it is asking to be taken on trust. Same
// markup as every other zuhd credit on the site (see pages/nyheter.astro), so
// the treatment is the site's own and not a one-off.
//
// It renders a frame and nothing else: the rows arrive from
// `src/scripts/nyheter.ts`, which fetches zuhd's Swedish payload in the browser.
// The column is `hidden` until there is something in it — an empty frame with a
// heading and no content is a dead affordance, and the reader has no way to tell
// it apart from a broken page.
import SectionLink from "./SectionLink.astro";
import "../styles/nyheter.css";
---

<aside id="nyheter-rail" class="nyheter-rail" aria-labelledby="nyheter-rail-rubrik" hidden>
	<h2 class="label sidolinje-rubrik" id="nyheter-rail-rubrik">Senaste nytt</h2>
	<ol class="nyheter-lista" data-nyheter="rail" aria-busy="true"></ol>
	<p class="nyheter-rail-fot">
		<SectionLink class="label arrow-link" href="/nyheter/">Alla nyheter</SectionLink>
	</p>
	<p class="label nyheter-avsandare">
		Nyheterna kommer från <a href="https://zuhd.news/" rel="noopener">zuhd.news</a>
	</p>
</aside>

<style>
	/* There is no height reservation here, and that is a decision rather than an
	   omission.

	   The obvious move is to reserve the rows so the page does not move when the
	   fetch lands. It does not survive contact with the two readers who matter
	   most: reserving means the column is in the layout before anyone knows
	   whether there is news, so a reader with JavaScript off — or with a content
	   blocker that stops a cross-origin request to a `.news` domain, which is not
	   hypothetical on this machine — is left looking at a headed, permanently
	   empty column on the front page of a reference site. An empty frame is a dead
	   affordance: nothing tells them it is a quiet day rather than a broken page.

	   In the rail this costs less than it did as a full-width band: the column
	   sits in its own grid track, so when it appears the movements beside it do
	   not move at all — only the track it occupies fills in. */
	/* A provenance note, not a label. It was set at --step--3 in tracked caps —
	   the same rung and the same register as "ALLA NYHETER →" — so a credit and
	   an action sat side by side asking for equal attention, and the foot of the
	   column ended on two lines of shouting. Sentence case at the smallest rung
	   is what a footnote looks like. */
	.nyheter-avsandare {
		margin-top: var(--space-half);
		font-size: var(--step--5);
		text-transform: none;
		letter-spacing: 0;
		color: var(--color-muted);
	}
</style>

<script>
	// The rail is client-rendered — nothing on the front page is edge-injected,
	// because this is the site's most important document and it does not get a
	// fetch-dependent Function in front of it. Without this import the column
	// ships as an empty, permanently hidden frame.
	import "../scripts/nyheter";
</script>
